Ankylosing Spondylitis
Rheumatology & Immunology
Inflammatory back pain, Bilateral sacroiliitis, HLA-B27 positivity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RHI-002
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe, unremitting lower back stiffness and pain, radiating to the buttocks, causing inability to sleep or mobilize safely within the home environment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe, immobilizing axial pain with acute functional decline, unable to be managed with over-the-counter analgesia. An urgent after-hours physical assessment was required to exclude acute spinal cord compression, cauda equina syndrome, or epidural abscess, and to prescribe appropriate medical therapy to prevent further functional deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower back is completely locked up; the pain wakes me up in the second half of the night, and it takes me hours to get moving in the morning."
History of Present Illness: Patient details an insidious onset of lower back stiffness originating before age 40, which has intermittently flared. Over the past 72 hours, the patient reports a severe exacerbation of inflammatory back pain worsening with rest and improving only slightly with physical activity. Patient reports profound morning stiffness lasting longer than 60 minutes. Pain is described as a deep ache with alternating buttock pain. Additionally notes recent onset of asymmetrical peripheral oligoarthritis in the right knee, compounding mobility issues. Pain escalates at night, severely disrupting sleep architecture.
Functional Status: Currently restricted to minimal indoor ambulation; struggling with activities of daily living due to axial rigidity.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of falls due to biomechanical stiffness and fatigue from sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/90 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Appears fatigued, maintaining a rigid, guarded posture when transitioning from sitting to standing.
Physical Exam: Musculoskeletal: Progressive loss of spinal mobility across multiple planes noted; positive modified Schober's test indicating restricted lumbar flexion. Exaggerated thoracic kyphosis observed. Bilateral sacroiliac joint tenderness elicited upon direct palpation and Faber testing. Asymmetrical peripheral oligoarthritis noted with mild effusion in the right knee. Point tenderness over the Achilles tendon insertion consistent with enthesitis. Neurological: Normal tone, power, and reflexes in lower limbs; normal perianal sensation. Diagnostic Tetrad components clinically evident: inflammatory back pain, signs pointing toward bilateral sacroiliitis, and clinical suspicion for HLA-B27 positivity pending future testing.
Environmental Observations: Patient navigating stairs with extreme difficulty; no assistive devices present.
Medication Reconciliation: Using intermittent ibuprofen with inadequate dosing and minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Ankylosing Spondylitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Cauda Equina Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal perianal sensation, normal lower limb neurology, and absence of bowel or bladder incontinence.

Mechanical lower back pain - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain worsens with rest and improves with activity, presence of prolonged morning stiffness, and bilateral sacroiliac tenderness.

Septic discitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever, lack of intravenous drug use history, and chronic, insidious timeline of baseline symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with an acute, severe exacerbation of chronic axial and gluteal pain, leading to significant functional immobilization and sleep deprivation. Given the unremitting nature of the pain and functional decline, an urgent after-hours assessment was imperative to exclude neurological compromise, such as cauda equina syndrome, and to instigate an effective, optimized anti-inflammatory regimen. Leaving the patient unassessed and unmedicated until the next standard consultation period posed a definitive risk of prolonged immobilization, falls, and severe psychological distress, definitively meeting the criteria for MBS 585-600.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Initiate a regular, scheduled dosing regimen of a high-dose NSAID (e.g., Naproxen or Indomethacin) combined with a proton pump inhibitor for gastric protection. Recommend formal pelvic imaging and HLA-B27 genetic testing via the primary care provider.
Positive Behavioral Change: Consistent movement schedule: Set an alarm to walk for 5 minutes every two hours during the day; achieving this will interrupt prolonged static posturing, leading directly to decreased stiffness and proving the mechanical benefit of movement. Heat therapy application: Apply a hot pack to the lower back upon waking for 15 minutes to actively reduce morning rigidity, reinforcing self-management efficacy through immediate symptomatic relief.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral for targeted axial mobility exercises.
DME/Safety (Optional): Heating pad.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 1 week to assess NSAID response and organize imaging.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department immediately if you develop any loss of bowel or bladder control, numbness in the saddle or groin region, or sudden weakness/numbness radiating down both legs.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis represents a clinical exacerbation of an underlying inflammatory condition; definitive confirmation requires specialized imaging and rheumatological evaluation.
Clinical Course: Condition typically follows a relapsing-remitting course. Regular, targeted exercise and optimized anti-inflammatory therapy strongly favor functional preservation and symptom control.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest ED immediately if you experience urinary retention, loss of bowel control, or numbness around your genitals/buttocks, as these suggest dangerous nerve compression.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Inflammatory back pain requires continuous suppression of the immune response locally; scheduling NSAIDs around the clock, rather than "as needed," ensures medication levels remain high enough to break the pain-stiffness cycle.
Actionable Steps: Take NSAIDs strictly as prescribed with food. Perform gentle, frequent mobilization to keep the joints from fusing and stiffening.

